# Pipeline working directories.
# `*/temp/` only matched one level down, so paths like
# etl/collect/collect_espn_games/temp/ slipped through and 2,478 scraped JSON
# files ended up tracked. `**/temp/` matches at any depth.
**/temp/
**/logs/
**/backups/
**/staging_for_s3/

# Bulk scrape output
**/gamejsons/
**/pbpjsons/

# Generated data and experiment records
*.csv*
*.txt*

# Analysis and backtest outputs.
# `/analysis/` excluded the directory itself, and git cannot re-include a file
# whose parent directory is excluded - so no negation below could reach inside
# it. `/analysis/*` excludes the contents instead, which negations can override.
/analysis/*

# Windows alternate data streams, created when a file is downloaded via a browser
*:Zone.Identifier

# Python
*__pycache__*
*.pyc

# Environments and editors
/ncaaf_env
.idea*
.spyproject/

# schedule scrape backups
*.csv.bak-*

# collected CFBD player data
etl/collect/collect_cfbd_players/temp/

# comparison build from the garbage-time test
etl/summarize/results_with_garbage/

etl/summarize/results_a*/

# ==============================================================================
# Exceptions: things that SHIP, or that a README points at as evidence
# ==============================================================================
# The blanket *.csv* and *.txt* rules are right for generated data - the
# pipeline produces gigabytes of it. They were also swallowing the artefacts
# that ARE the model, which meant the numbers actually in production were
# unversioned: a bad refit could not be diffed, reverted, or even noticed.
#
# Negations must come after the rules they override; git takes the last
# matching pattern. Anything under a **/temp/ directory stays ignored either
# way, because that directory is excluded rather than its contents.

# The blend weights that ship. 17 rows, and the refit on 2026-08-18 moved every
# one of them - exactly the change that should have been reviewable as a diff.
!model_training/model_blender/blended_model.csv

# The walk-forward history those weights are fitted on. Regenerating it retrains
# both models once per season, so this is hours of compute, and it is the only
# way to see whether a weight moved because the evidence moved.
!/analysis/backtest_expanding_preds.csv

# Cited by research/README.md as where a finding lives; an unversioned file a
# README points at is a broken reference for anyone who clones.
!/analysis/preseason_lag_ablation.csv
!/analysis/encoding_experiment.csv

# The experiment files record model_params, data_features, train_season_range
# and the test split - the entire specification of a trained model. They were
# ignored, inside directories that are themselves untracked, which put the
# hyperparameters in a worse place than the weights.
#
# create_experiment.sh reads the newest of these to carry parameters forward on
# a routine retrain. Those parameters are worth 1.265 MAE over library defaults
# (research/in_season_hyperparams.sh), so if the run directories are ever
# cleaned, the next retrain silently loses that much and reports nothing.
!model_training/**/*experiment*.txt
!model_training/**/model_results.txt
